/-!
# The twists of the exceptional curve are globally generated and nontrivial

Let `(A, 𝔪, κ)` be as in Situation [Stacks, Tag 0B4W], let `X` be the blowup of `Spec(A)` in `𝔪`
and let `E ⊆ X` be the exceptional divisor. This file proves the two properties of `𝒪_E(n)` that
[Stacks, Tag 0B62] asks of the invertible module it is applied to, and draws the consequence for
the exceptional curve.

* `𝒪_E(n)` **is generated by its global sections** for every `n ≥ 0`. The ideal sheaf
  `𝓘 = 𝔪𝒪_X` of `E` is generated by its global sections ([Stacks, Tag 0B4Y], as
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RationalSingularitySituation.exceptionalIdealGeneratingSections`), hence so
  is every power `𝓘ⁿ`, and `𝒪_E(n)` is the restriction `i^*𝓘ⁿ` by part (3) of
  [Stacks, Tag 02OS]. Pulling generating sections back along `i` is Mathlib's
  `SheafOfModules.GeneratingSections.map`, whose two inputs — that `i^*` preserves colimits and
  carries `𝒪_X` to `𝒪_E` — are the adjunction `i^* ⊣ i_*` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.pullbackUnitIso`.

* `𝒪_E(n)` **is not isomorphic to `𝒪_E`** for `n ≥ 1`. Were it, `H⁰(E, 𝒪_E(n))` would be
  `H⁰(E, 𝒪_E) = κ`, a line. But clause (5) of [Stacks, Tag 0B63] identifies `H⁰(E, 𝒪_E(n))`
  with `𝔪ⁿ/𝔪ⁿ⁺¹`, and in a Noetherian local ring of dimension two that graded piece has
  dimension at least two: `𝔪ⁿ` has height two, so Krull's height theorem forbids it from being
  generated by one element. This is
  `Stacks.KrullDimension.two_le_finrank_maximalIdealPow_quotient`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.BlowUpDualizing.generatingSectionsCurveTwistSheaf`: `𝒪_E(n)` is generated
  by its global sections, for every `n ≥ 0` and with no hypothesis beyond Situation 0B4W.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BlowUpDualizing.two_le_finrank_cohomologyOverField_curveTwistSheaf_zero`:
  `dim_κ H⁰(E, 𝒪_E(n)) ≥ 2` for `n ≥ 1`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BlowUpDualizing.not_isTrivial_curveTwistSheaf`: `𝒪_E(n) ≇ 𝒪_E` for
  `n ≥ 1`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BlowUpDualizing.isZero_firstCohomology_curveTwist_of_dualityVanishing`:
  **`H¹(E, ω_E(n+1)) = 0` for every `n ≥ 0`**, for a module `ω_E` on `E` for which duality holds
  in the form `AlgebraicGeometry.CohenMacaulayCurveSituation.DualityVanishing`. This is
  [Stacks, Tag 0B62] applied to the exceptional curve: the two properties above are exactly its
  hypotheses on `𝒪_E(n+1)`, and Situation [Stacks, Tag 0B5D] at `E` is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.cohenMacaulayCurveSituationExceptionalDivisor`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BlowUpDualizing.isZero_firstCohomology_dualizingTwist_of_dualityVanishing`:
  the same substitution made inside
  `AlgebraicGeometry.BlowUpDualizing.isZero_firstCohomology_dualizingTwist_of_curveVanishing`,
  so that clause (2) of [Stacks, Tag 0B64] is reduced to clause (1) of that tag and duality for
  `ω_E`.

## What `DualityVanishing` is, and why it cannot be dropped

`DualityVanishing ω` says that `H⁰(X, ℒ^{⊗ -1}) = 0` forces `H¹(X, ω ⊗ ℒ)= 0` for invertible
`ℒ`; it is the form [Stacks, Tag 0B62] consumes duality in, and it pins `ω` down. It is **not** a
placeholder for an absent duality — it has two producers, and the duality it is an instance of is
proved:

* `AlgebraicGeometry.CohenMacaulayCurveSituation.dualityVanishing_dualizingSheafOverField` of
  `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Curves/VanishingResults/DualizingTwist.lean` proves it with no
  hypothesis, in every Situation [Stacks, Tag 0B5D] and so in particular at the exceptional
  curve, for the single module `Stacks.Duality.dualizingSheafOverField S.f` — the canonical
  dualizing sheaf `H^{-1}(f^×(𝒪_{Spec κ}))`. Its inputs are
  `Stacks.Duality.subsingleton_zExt_one_dualizingSheafOverField_of_subsingleton`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.zExtInverseTensorAddEquiv` and
  `Stacks.Duality.subsingleton_cohomologyOverField_of_subsingleton_zExt`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BlowUpDualizing.dualityVanishing_of_iso_dualizingSheafOverField` of
  `…/RationalSingularities/BlowUpExceptionalDualizing.lean` transports it along an isomorphism
  with that sheaf, the predicate being a condition on the module alone.

Clause (4) of [Stacks, Tag 0BS2], the duality `Ext^i_X(K, ω_X^∙) = Hom_k(H^{-i}(X, K), k)`, is
likewise proved, `k`-linearly, as `Stacks.Duality.extDualityLinearEquiv`; [Stacks, Tag 0BS3] and
[Stacks, Tag 0BS4] have registrations too, in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Curves/VanishingResults/DualizingDimensionDuality.lean`,
`…/DualizingDimensionDualityBundle.lean` and
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Curves/DualizingInvertibleDimensionDuality.lean`. All of them, and
both producers above, speak of the *canonical* dualizing complex or sheaf of the curve; none of
them says anything about an arbitrary dualizing module. See the module documentation of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Curves/VanishingResults/DualizingTwist.lean`.

**So what `DualityVanishing ω_E` remains for is the gap between an arbitrary `ω_E` and the
canonical one.** The `ω_E` the results below are about is constrained only by
`Stacks.Duality.IsDualizingModule` and by clause (1) of [Stacks, Tag 0B64], and neither producer
applies to it, because nothing here identifies it with
`Stacks.Duality.dualizingSheafOverField ((cohenMacaulayCurveSituationExceptionalDivisor S).f)`.
That isomorphism is the missing declaration; it is what
`AlgebraicGeometry.BlowUpDualizing.dualityVanishing_of_iso_dualizingSheafOverField` consumes, and
it is strictly stronger than the hypotheses available, as the counterexample in the next
paragraph shows.

**It cannot be replaced by `Stacks.Duality.IsDualizingModule ω_E`**, which is what
`AlgebraicGeometry.BlowUpDualizing.isZero_firstCohomology_dualizingTwist_of_curveVanishing`
assumes about `ω_E`. That predicate is invariant under tensoring by an invertible module, while
the conclusion `H¹(E, ω_E(n+1)) = 0` is not. Take `A = k[x, y]` localized at `(x, y)`, a regular
local ring of dimension two, so that `X` is the blowup of the plane at a point and `E ≅ ℙ¹_k`
with `𝒪_E(1) = 𝒪_{ℙ¹}(1)`. The structure sheaf `𝒪_X` is a dualizing complex on the regular
scheme `X`, and so is `𝒪_X(N)` for every `N`, because tensoring a dualizing complex by an
invertible module gives a dualizing complex. Taking `ω_X = 𝒪_X(-5)` and
`ω_E = ω_X|_E ⊗ 𝒪_E(-1) = 𝒪_{ℙ¹}(-6)` satisfies every other hypothesis of that theorem —
`ω_X` and `ω_E` are dualizing modules and the clause-(1) isomorphism `e` holds by construction —
while `H¹(E, ω_E(1)) = H¹(ℙ¹, 𝒪(-5))` has dimension four. So the vanishing genuinely needs a
normalization of `ω_E`, and `DualityVanishing` supplies one.

**Normalizing `ω_X` does not supply one.** The condition [Stacks, Remark 0B4R] imposes on *the*
dualizing module of `X` is available as
`AlgebraicGeometry.RationalSingularitySituation.NormalizedDualizingModule`, and it is relative
duality in degree zero: it does not separate `ω_X` from `ω_X(E)`, so it does not determine
`ω_X|_E` and cannot determine `ω_E`. The implementation notes of
`…/RationalSingularities/NormalizedDualizingModule.lean` exhibit a non-regular `A` for which
`𝒪_X` and `𝒪_X(E)` are both normalized, the second with `H¹(X, 𝒪_X(E)) ≠ 0` and restricting on
`E` to `𝒪_{ℙ¹}(-2)`, whose first cohomology is nonzero too. Duality for `ω_E` is therefore
independent of the normalization of `ω_X`, and what would discharge it is the isomorphism of
`ω_E` with `Stacks.Duality.dualizingSheafOverField ((cohenMacaulayCurveSituationExceptionalDivisor
S).f)` named above — not a further tag of the duality itself, [Stacks, Tag 0BS4] being registered
already in `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Curves/DualizingInvertibleDimensionDuality.lean`.

